# buffer overflow 0

**Category:** Binary Exploitation
**Difficulty:** Medium
**Authors:** Alex Fulton / Palash Oswal
**Event:** picoCTF 2022

## Challenge Description

This was my first buffer overflow challenge.

The program reads user input and eventually copies it into a fixed-size buffer:

```c
void vuln(char *input){
    char buf2[16];
    strcpy(buf2, input);
}
```

The interesting part is that `buf2` can only hold 16 bytes, but `strcpy()` does not check the length of the input.

The program also registers a custom handler for segmentation faults:

```c
void sigsegv_handler(int sig) {
    printf("%s\n", flag);
    fflush(stdout);
    exit(1);
}
```

and:

```c
signal(SIGSEGV, sigsegv_handler);
```

This means that if we can cause the program to crash with a segmentation fault, the signal handler will print the flag for us.

## Finding the Vulnerability

The main function reads input using:

```c
char buf1[100];
gets(buf1);
```

Then the input is passed to:

```c
vuln(buf1);
```

Inside `vuln()`, it is copied into a much smaller buffer:

```c
char buf2[16];
strcpy(buf2, input);
```

There is no bounds checking here.

So if the input is longer than `buf2`, `strcpy()` continues writing past the end of the buffer and starts overwriting nearby stack memory.

For this challenge, I do not need to control the return address or redirect execution to another function. I only need to make the program crash.

## Exploit

I connected to the remote service and first tested a short input:

```text
Input: 2
The program will exit now
```

Nothing happens because the input fits inside the buffer.

Then I sent a much longer input.

The long string overflows `buf2`, corrupts the stack, and eventually causes a segmentation fault.

Because the program installed `sigsegv_handler()`, the crash causes the flag to be printed:

```text
Input: AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA...
picoCTF{ov3rfl0ws_ar3nt_that_bad_9f2364bc}
```

## What I Learned

The vulnerable code is:

```c
char buf2[16];
strcpy(buf2, input);
```

`strcpy()` copies data until it reaches a null byte and does not know how large the destination buffer is.

If the input is larger than the destination, it can overwrite memory outside the buffer.

This challenge is simpler than a typical buffer overflow exploit because we are not trying to gain control of the instruction pointer. The program itself turns a crash into a win condition by printing the flag whenever `SIGSEGV` occurs.

So the exploit is simply:

```text
send enough data -> overflow buf2 -> cause SIGSEGV -> handler prints flag
```

## Conclusion

This was a simple challenge, but it was useful as my first introduction to buffer overflows.

The important idea is that writing more data than a fixed-size buffer can hold may corrupt nearby memory and crash the program.

Later buffer overflow challenges usually require more control, such as overwriting the saved return address, but this challenge only requires causing a segmentation fault.

